  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "For an array AAA of length NNN, the integer KKK is said to be AAA-good if the following condition holds:\n\n\ngcd⁡(Ai,K)=gcd⁡(Aj,K)\\gcd(A_i, K) = \\gcd(A_j, K)gcd(Ai​,K)=gcd(Aj​,K) for every 1≤i&lt;j≤N1 \\leq i \\lt j \\leq N1≤i&lt;j≤N.\nDefine f(A)f(A)f(A) to be the number of integers between 111 and MMM that are AAA-good.\n\nYou are given integers NNN and MMM. Compute the sum of f(A)f(A)f(A) across all MNM^NMN arrays AAA of length NNN whose elements lie between 111 and MMM.\n\nThis sum can be large, so compute it modulo 998244353998244353998244353.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the length of the array and the maximum allowed element.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the sum of f(A)f(A)f(A) across all MNM^NMN arrays, modulo 998244353998244353998244353.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N≤5⋅1051 \\leq N \\leq 5\\cdot 10^51≤N≤5⋅105",
      "1≤M≤5⋅1051 \\leq M \\leq 5\\cdot 10^51≤M≤5⋅105",
      "The sum of NNN and the sum of MMM over all test cases both won't exceed 5⋅1055\\cdot 10^55⋅105."
    ],
